          %p
            Poincaré duality over some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalence
            (if it exists) relating
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology"} homology
            on that space.
          %p
            The canonical example is the Poincaré duality in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
            = succeed "/" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi H
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^\bullet(X)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+homology"} ordinary homology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_\bullet(X)
            which exists over an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation"} orientable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+manifold"} closed manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/volume+form"} volume form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            induces by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cap+product"} cap product
            an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∩
                  %mi ω
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ≃
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (-)\cap \omega \;\colon\; H_\bullet(X) \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} H^{n-\bullet}(X)
                  \\,.
          %p
            More generally Poincaré duality is about
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+objects"} dual objects
            in a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+cohomology+theory"} generalized cohomology theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            above is furthermore
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-orientation"} K-oriented
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation+in+generalized+cohomology"} oriented
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+theory"} cohomology theory
            (hence if it has
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin%5Ec+structure"} spin^c structure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-homology"} K-homology
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theory
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+duality+algebra"} Poincaré duality algebra
          %p
            Poincaré duality is the mechanism behind
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Umkehr+maps"} Umkehr maps
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/push-forward+in+generalized+cohomology"} push-forward in generalized cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces"} spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon X \to Y
            which enjoy Poincaré duality with respect to some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+cohomology+theory"} generalized cohomology theory
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msup
                    %mi R
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi R
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  f^\ast \colon R^\bullet(Y) \to R^\bullet(X)
          %p
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+morphism"} dual morphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msup
                    %mi R
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %msup
                    %mi R
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  f_! \colon R^\bullet(X) \simeq R_\bullet(X) \stackrel{f_\ast}{\to} R_\bullet(Y) \simeq R^\bullet(Y)
                  \\,.
          %p Here the duality is typically exhibited in two steps:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Atiyah+duality"} Atiyah duality
                identifies the dual of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi R
                        %mo •
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R^\bullet(X)
                with the
                = succeed "-cohomology" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thom+space"} Thom space
                of
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thom+isomorphism"} Thom isomorphism
                identified the
                = succeed "-cohomology" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            More generally, spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            are not
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-dual"} self-dual
            in this way, but may at least be dual to themselves but equipped with a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"} twist
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+Umkehr+maps"} twisted Umkehr maps
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"} twisted cohomology
            (see for instance at
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freed-Witten-Kapustin+anomaly+cancellation"} Freed-Witten-Kapustin anomaly cancellation
          %p
            For more on this see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+Umkehr+map"} twisted Umkehr map
          %h2#DefinitionAndStatement Definition and statement
          %p We first state the
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#TraditionalFormulation"} Traditional formulation
          %p and then its
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a{:href => "#RefinementInHomotopyTheory"} Refinement in homotopy theory
              \.
          %h3#TraditionalFormulation Traditional formulation
          %p
            Around 1895
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Henri+Poincar%C3%A9"} Henri Poincaré
            made an observation about
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Betti+numbers"} Betti numbers
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+manifolds"} closed manifolds
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eduard+%C4%8Cech"} Eduard Čech
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hassler+Whitney"} Hassler Whitney
            in the following modern form:
          #TraditionalPoincareDuality.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+manifold"} closed manifold
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              that is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation"} orientable
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cap+product"} cap product
              with any choice of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation"} orientation
              – in the form of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+class"} fundamental class
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi H
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [X] \in H_n(X)
              – induces
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphisms"} isomorphisms
              of the form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∩
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi H
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (-)\cap [X] \;\colon\; H^k(X) \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} H_{n-k}(X)
            %p
              between the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
              and the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+homology"} ordinary homology
              groups of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Later this was turned around and more general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            satisfying this condition were considered:
          #PoincareDualitySpace.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              for which there is
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \in \mathbb{N}
              and a class
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi H
                      %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [X] \in H_d(X)
              such that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cap+product"} cap product
              induces
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphisms"} isomorphisms
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∩
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi H
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mo −
                        %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (-) \cap [X] \;\colon\; H^\bullet(X) \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} H_{d-\bullet}(X)
            %p
              is called a
              = succeed "." do
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+duality+space"} Poincaré duality space
          %h3#RefinementInHomotopyTheory Refinement to homotopy theory
          %p
            Traditionally Poincaré duality is stated as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+object"} duality
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+homology"} chain
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology+groups"} homology groups
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complexes"} chain complexes
            to their
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology+groups"} homology groups
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            to just some invariants, however forgets a lot of information. But it turns out that this can always be lifted:
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+duality+space"} Poincaré duality space
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PoincareDualitySpace"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphism"} quasi-isomorphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi Σ
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mi d
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    C^\bullet(X) \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} \Sigma^{-d} C_\bullet(X)
            %p
              between the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              with that
              = succeed "-fold" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/suspension"} de-suspension
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+homology"} ordinary homology
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %p
              This is such that its image in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+homology"} chain homology
              is (up to sign) the traditional Poincaré duality isomorphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi H
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mo −
                        %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    H^\bullet(X) \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} H_{d -\bullet}(X)
            %p
              of theorem
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TraditionalPoincareDuality"}
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo •
                      %mo =
                      %mi r
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bullet=r
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %msup
                        %mn 1
                        %mrow
                          %mi r
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi r
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -1^{r(r+1)/2}
          %p
            This is (
            %a{:href => "#EM11"}> EM 11, theorem, 2.5.2
            ).
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              Since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi 𝕀
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∨
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\bullet(X) \simeq [C_\bullet(X,), \mathbb{I}] \simeq (C_\bullet(X))^\vee
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+object"} dual object
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_\bullet(X)
              in the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+of+unbounded+chain+complexes"} (∞,1)-category of unbounded chain complexes
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+duality+space"} Poincaré duality space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_\bullet(X)
              is almost a self-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+objects"}> dual objects
              , except for a degree-shift, hence except for a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"} twist
              of (itself) degree 0.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#recognition_of_manifolds Recognition of manifolds
          .query
            %p From [ALGTOP-L], Oct 5, 2010.
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jim+Stasheff"}> Jim Stasheff
                  \:
                %p Any one have a reference for obstructions which detect whether a space whose cohomology has Poincare duality is actually a manifold? thanks
              %li
                %p John Klein:
                %p Ranicki’s total surgery obstruction does it in the top case, in surgery dimensions.
                %ul
                  %li
                    The total surgery obstruction. Algebraic topology, Aarhus 1978 (Proc. Sympos., Univ. Aarhus, Aarhus, 1978), pp. 275–316, Lecture Notes in Math., 763, Springer, Berlin, 1979. (
                    %a{:href => "http://www.maths.ed.ac.uk/~aar/papers/total.pdf"}> pdf
                    )
              %li
                %p Nathanien Rounds:
                %p I believe this question is answered (simply connected case, over Q) in Dennis Sullivan’s paper Infinitesmal Computations in Topology (Theorem 13.2). The answer, as I understand it, is that outside dimension 4k any graded commutative algebra over Q wtih first betti number 0 satisfying Poincare Duality can be realized as the cohomoloyg ring of a manifold. In dimension 4k there is an obstruction related to the signature which is given in that paper. There won’t in general be a unique manifold corresponding to the ring; for example one can choose different rational Pontragin classes and change the homemorphism type but not the cohomology.
                %p The general answer (not simply connected, over Z) is given by Ranicki’s total surgery obstruction, as John Klein has already pointed out. One way to interpret this obstruction geometrically is that the Poincare duality map is always “local” but it need not have a “local” inverse, and the lack of a local inverse is an obstruction to having a anifold structure. See for example McCrory’s paper “A Characterization of Homology Manifolds” and also my thesis, which if you’re interested is here:
                %p www.math.purdue.edu/~nrounds
          %p
            See also (
            %a{:href => "#Ranicki96"}> Ranicki 96
            ) and see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+complex"} Poincaré complex
          %h3#relation_to_thom_isomorphism Relation to Thom isomorphism
          %p
            The Poincaré dual of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/submanifold"} submanifold
            can be identified with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thom+class"} Thom class
            on its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+bundle"} normal bundle
          %p (…)
          %h3#relation_to_pushforward_in_cohomology Relation to push-forward in cohomology
          %p
            Given Poincare duality and hence (twisted-)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-dual+objects"}> self-dual objects
            , the
            %a{:href => "self-dual+object#RelationToDaggerCompactStructure"} induced dagger-structure
            allows torevert morphisms in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Umkehr+maps"} Umkehr maps
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+integration"} fiber integration
            in cohomology.
          %h3#generalizations Generalizations
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/six+operations"} six operations
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+duality"} Grothendieck duality
            are designed to ensure a generalized and relative versions of Poincaré duality and related phenomena in the setups like sheaf and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+duality"} Grothendieck duality
            for references.
          %p
            Another generalization, for singular spaces, is with help of stratifications and via
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersection+cohomology"} intersection cohomology
          %h2#variants_and_generalizations Variants and generalizations
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                in
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+duality+complex"} Poincaré duality complex
            %li
              %p
                in
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+topology"} noncommutative topology
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+duality+algebra"} Poincaré duality algebra
            %li
              %p
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category"} derived
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf"} abelian
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+and+topos+theory"} sheaf and topos theory
                (for
                = succeed "):" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} abelian sheaf cohomology
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Verdier+duality"} Verdier duality
            %li
              %p
                in
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+cohomology"} nonabelian cohomology
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+Poincar%C3%A9+duality"} nonabelian Poincaré duality
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+complex"} Poincaré complex
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+duality+algebra"} Poincaré duality algebra
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KO-dimension"} KO-dimension
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Serre+duality"} Serre duality
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p
            This historical work of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Henri+Poincar%C3%A9"} Henri Poincaré
            is reviewed around page 28. pf
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean+Dieudonn%C3%A9"}> Jean Dieudonné
              ,
              %em A History of Algebraic and Differential Topology, 1900 - 1960
          %p Summaries of the traditional modern statement of Poincaré duality are for instance in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eom"}> eom
                ,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://www.encyclopediaofmath.org/index.php/Poincar%C3%A9_duality"} Poincaré duality
            %li
              %p
                Manifold Atlas,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://www.map.mpim-bonn.mpg.de/Poincar%C3%A9_Duality_Spaces"} Poincaré Duality Spaces
          %p
            Discussion of generalizations to “chain duality” (see also at
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincare+complex"} Poincare complex
          %ul#Ranicki96
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrew+Ranicki"}> Andrew Ranicki
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em 45 slides on chain duality
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.uiuc.edu/K-theory/0154/45slides.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            Discussion in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/etale+cohomology"} etale cohomology
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/James+Milne"}> James Milne
              , sections 14 and 24 of
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lectures+on+%C3%89tale+Cohomology"} Lectures on Étale Cohomology
          %p
            With an eye towards generalization in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+geometry"} spectral geometry
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+triples"}> spectral triples
            ):
          %ul
            %li#Connes95
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"}> Alain Connes
              , page 10 of
              = succeed "," do
                %em Noncommutative geometry and reality
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.alainconnes.org/docs/reality.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            In the generality of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supermanifolds"} supermanifolds
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Konstantin+Eder"}> Konstantin Eder
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Huerta"} John Huerta
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simone+Noja"} Simone Noja
              %em Poincaré Duality for Supermanifolds, Higher Cartan Structures and Geometric Supergravity
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.05224"}> arXiv:2312.05224
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %h3#for_equivariant_cohomology For equivariant cohomology
          %p
            In
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+cohomology"} equivariant cohomology
          %p
            For
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+groups"} finite groups
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steven+Costenoble"}> Steven Costenoble
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stefan+Waner"} Stefan Waner
              = succeed "," do
                %em Equivariant Poincaré duality
          %p
            For
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+Lie+groups"} compact Lie groups
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michel+Brion"}> Michel Brion
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Poincaré duality and equivariant (co)homology
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.mmj/1030132709"} EUCLID
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steven+Costenoble"}> Steven Costenoble
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stefan+Waner"} Stefan Waner
                %em Equivariant ordinary homology and cohomology
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0310237"}> arXiv:math/0310237
                )
            %li
              %p
                Christopher Allday, Matthias Franz,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Volker+Puppe"} Volker Puppe
                %em Equivariant Poincaré-Alexander-Lefschetz duality and the Cohen-Macaulay property
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1303.1146"}> arXiv:1303.1146
                )
          %p
            In
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+K-theory"} equivariant K-theory
          %ul
            %li#Tu06
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Louis+Tu"}> Jean-Louis Tu
                ,
                %em Twisted K-theory and Poincare duality
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0609556"}> arXiv:0609556
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heath+Emerson"}> Heath Emerson
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ralf+Meyer"} Ralf Meyer
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Dualities in equivariant Kasparov theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0711.0025"} arXiv:0711.0025
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heath+Emerson"}> Heath Emerson
                ,
                %em Duality, correspondences and the Lefschetz map in equivariant KK-theory: a survey
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv-web3.library.cornell.edu/abs/0904.4744"}> arXiv:0904.4744
                )
          %h3#for_hochschild_cohomology For Hochschild cohomology
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+duality"} Poincaré duality
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hochschild+cohomology"} Hochschild (co)homology
          %ul#VanDenBergh
            %li
              %p
                M. Van den Bergh,
                %em A relation between Hochschild homology and cohomology for Gorenstein rings
                \. Proc. Amer. Math. Soc. 126 (1998), 1345–1348; (
                %a{:href => "http://www.jstor.org/stable/118786"}> JSTOR
                )
              %p Correction: Proc. Amer. Math. Soc. 130 (2002), 2809–2810.
          %p with more on that in
          %ul
            %li
              U. Krähmer,
              %em Poincaré duality in Hochschild cohomology
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.maths.gla.ac.uk/~ukraehmer/brussels.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            That this Poincare duality takes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Connes+coboundary+operator"} Connes coboundary operator
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV+operator"} BV operator
            is shown in
          %ul#Ginzburg
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Victor+Ginzburg"}> Victor Ginzburg
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Calabi-Yau Algebras
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.AG/0612139"} arXiv/math.AG/0612139
          %h3#for_spaces_in_higher_geometry For spaces in higher geometry
          %p
            Poincaré duality on
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne-Mumford+stacks"} Deligne-Mumford stacks
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifolds"} orbifolds
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Dan Abramovich, Tom Graber,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Angelo+Vistoli"} Angelo Vistoli
                %em Gromov-Witten theory of Deligne-Mumford stacks
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0603151"}> arXiv:math/0603151
                )
            %li
              %p
                Weimin Chen,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yongbin+Ruan"} Yongbin Ruan
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A New Cohomology Theory for Orbifold
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0004129"} arXiv:math/0004129
          %p
            For disucssion in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+topology"} noncommutative topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-theory"} KK-theory
            see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+duality+algebra"} Poincaré duality algebra
          %h3#on_the_level_of_chains On the level of chains
          %p
            Poincaré duality is traditional considered on the level of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+groups"} cohomology groups
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complexes"} chain complexes
          %p Such “derived” Poincaré duality on the level of chain complexes is claimed in
          %ul
            %li#EM11
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eric+J.+Malm"}> Eric J. Malm
              , theorem 2.5.2 of:
              %em String topology and the based loop space
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1103.6198"}> arXiv:1103.6198
              , slides:
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://math.ucr.edu/~jbergner/ucr-st-present.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Malm-BasedLoopStringTopology.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p based on – or at least inspired by – the discussion in (section 10) of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Dwyer"}> William Dwyer
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Greenlees"} John Greenlees
              %em Duality in algebra and topology
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0510247"}> arXiv:math/0510247
              )
          %p The article
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomas+Tradler"}> Thomas Tradler
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mahmoud+Zeinalian"} Mahmoud Zeinalian
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dennis+Sullivan"} Dennis Sullivan
              %em Infinity Structure of Poincare Duality Spaces
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0309455"}> arXiv:math/0309455
              )
          %p
            claims a chain duality (even of
            = succeed "-comodule" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\infty
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bimodule"} bimodule
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+chain+complex"} singular chain complex
            of any Poincaré duality topological space.
          %p A review is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomas+Tradler"}> Thomas Tradler
              ,
              %em Infinity inner products on A-infinity algebras
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0806.0065"}> arXiv:0806.0065
              )
        .revisedby
          %p
            Last revised on September 26, 2025 at 17:40:30.
            See the
